1
00:00:00,880 --> 00:00:05,140
Imagine putting away R3,833 a month.

2
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:10,140
In about eleven years you stop… and your
money just keeps growing, for the rest of

3
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:11,580
your working life.

4
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:15,207
And SARS never takes a
single cent of that growth.

5
00:00:15,227 --> 00:00:16,353
That's not a trick.

6
00:00:16,373 --> 00:00:17,420
It's the law.

7
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:22,140
It's called a Tax-Free Savings Account,
and many South Africans either don't have

8
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:24,460
one… or aren't using it properly.

9
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:29,500
In this video, you'll learn exactly how it
works, the two mistakes that cost people

10
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:32,940
money, and how to open one, step by step.

11
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:34,540
Let's start from zero.

12
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:37,900
First, what is a Tax-Free Savings Account?

13
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:40,327
It's not a special type of investment.

14
00:00:40,347 --> 00:00:41,953
Think of it as a container.

15
00:00:41,973 --> 00:00:43,020
A wrapper.

16
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:47,300
Inside the wrapper, you can hold different
things: a simple savings account at your

17
00:00:47,320 --> 00:00:51,980
bank, a unit trust, or an ETF
that follows the stock market.

18
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,087
The wrapper's job is simple.

19
00:00:54,107 --> 00:00:58,007
Whatever your money earns
inside it is protected from tax.

20
00:00:58,027 --> 00:01:04,180
TFSAs started on 1 March 2015, and today
most banks and investment platforms in

21
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:05,900
South Africa offer one.

22
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:08,673
So which taxes do you avoid?

23
00:01:08,693 --> 00:01:11,340
Normally, your money
gets taxed in three ways.

24
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:15,447
Interest is taxed as income, once
it's above your yearly exemption.

25
00:01:15,467 --> 00:01:19,820
That's R23,800 a year if you're under 65.

26
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:23,100
Dividends from shares are taxed at 20%.

27
00:01:23,120 --> 00:01:27,500
And when an investment grows and you sell
it, you can pay capital gains tax on the

28
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:30,300
profit above R50,000 a year.

29
00:01:30,320 --> 00:01:33,740
Inside a TFSA, none of that applies.

30
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:35,500
No income tax on interest.

31
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:36,980
No dividends tax.

32
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:38,727
No capital gains tax.

33
00:01:38,747 --> 00:01:45,420
And here's a bonus: TFSA interest doesn't
even use up your normal R23,800 interest

34
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:46,300
exemption.

35
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:48,460
You keep that for your other savings.

36
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:49,927
Let's make that real.

37
00:01:49,947 --> 00:01:54,967
Say you have R500,000 in a normal
savings account earning 8% a year.

38
00:01:54,987 --> 00:01:57,207
That's R40,000 of interest.

39
00:01:57,227 --> 00:02:03,580
The first R23,800 is exempt,
so R16,200 is taxable.

40
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:08,700
If you pay 26% tax, that's
about R4,200 to SARS.

41
00:02:08,720 --> 00:02:10,567
Every single year.

42
00:02:10,587 --> 00:02:13,953
Inside a TFSA, that bill is zero.

43
00:02:13,973 --> 00:02:15,500
Of course, there's a limit.

44
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:16,940
Actually, there are two.

45
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:18,140
The yearly limit.

46
00:02:18,160 --> 00:02:23,793
From 1 March 2026, you can put
in up to R46,000 per tax year.

47
00:02:23,813 --> 00:02:27,180
That's about R3,833 a month.

48
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:30,780
When TFSAs started, the limit was R30,000.

49
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:38,020
It went up to R33,000, then
R36,000, and now R46,000,

50
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:40,100
the biggest increase so far.

51
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:46,060
And the lifetime limit: R500,000
per person, for your whole life.

52
00:02:46,080 --> 00:02:48,060
One thing catches people out.

53
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:51,180
Unused space does not carry over.

54
00:02:51,520 --> 00:02:58,140
If you only put in R20,000 this tax
year, the other R26,000 is gone forever.

55
00:02:58,480 --> 00:03:02,260
Next year, you start again at R46,000.

56
00:03:02,320 --> 00:03:07,660
At the full amount, it takes about eleven
years to reach the R500,000 lifetime

57
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:08,540
limit.

58
00:03:09,276 --> 00:03:10,976
Now the best part.

59
00:03:10,996 --> 00:03:15,469
The limits only count what you
put in… not what it grows into.

60
00:03:15,489 --> 00:03:17,016
Let's look at an example.

61
00:03:17,036 --> 00:03:20,536
These numbers are only an
illustration, not a promise.

62
00:03:20,556 --> 00:03:22,723
Say you start at 25.

63
00:03:22,743 --> 00:03:29,416
You put in R3,833 a month until you
reach the R500,000 limit, at about

64
00:03:29,436 --> 00:03:30,856
age 36.

65
00:03:30,876 --> 00:03:34,483
Then you stop paying in
completely, and you just leave it.

66
00:03:34,503 --> 00:03:40,656
If it grows at 5% a year above inflation,
after fees, then by 65 you'd have about

67
00:03:40,676 --> 00:03:44,056
R2.7 million, in today's money.

68
00:03:44,076 --> 00:03:47,256
Only R500,000 of that came from you.

69
00:03:47,276 --> 00:03:52,776
About 82% is growth… and
every rand of it is tax-free.

70
00:03:52,796 --> 00:03:58,936
Start ten years later, at 35, and you'd
end up with about R1.7 million instead.

71
00:03:58,956 --> 00:04:00,376
Same contributions.

72
00:04:00,396 --> 00:04:02,593
Ten fewer years of growth.

73
00:04:02,613 --> 00:04:05,485
That's why starting early matters so much.

74
00:04:05,505 --> 00:04:07,176
But there are two traps.

75
00:04:07,196 --> 00:04:10,109
Trap number one: going over the limit.

76
00:04:10,129 --> 00:04:13,576
The limits are for you as
a person, not per account.

77
00:04:13,596 --> 00:04:17,976
You're allowed to have more than one TFSA,
but everything you put into all of them

78
00:04:17,996 --> 00:04:19,256
counts together.

79
00:04:19,276 --> 00:04:23,016
And your bank can only see its
own account, so it can't warn you.

80
00:04:23,036 --> 00:04:25,096
Keeping track is your job.

81
00:04:25,116 --> 00:04:29,523
If you go over, SARS charges
a 40% penalty on the extra.

82
00:04:29,543 --> 00:04:34,296
Here's SARS's own example: you
put in R50,000 in one tax year.

83
00:04:34,316 --> 00:04:38,856
The limit is R46,000,
so you're R4,000 over.

84
00:04:38,876 --> 00:04:43,576
Forty percent of R4,000
is a R1,600 penalty.

85
00:04:43,596 --> 00:04:45,896
Even a tiny mistake counts.

86
00:04:45,916 --> 00:04:52,856
A debit order of R3,834 a month
adds up to R46,008 for the year.

87
00:04:52,876 --> 00:04:55,016
That's R8 over the limit.

88
00:04:55,036 --> 00:04:57,923
Trap number two: taking money out.

89
00:04:57,943 --> 00:05:00,963
You can withdraw from a TFSA at any time.

90
00:05:00,983 --> 00:05:03,216
But the space you used is gone.

91
00:05:03,236 --> 00:05:08,616
If you take out R10,000 and later put it
back, SARS treats that as a new R10,000

92
00:05:08,636 --> 00:05:10,056
contribution.

93
00:05:10,076 --> 00:05:14,136
It counts against your yearly limit
and your lifetime limit again.

94
00:05:14,156 --> 00:05:17,416
So a TFSA is not your emergency fund.

95
00:05:17,436 --> 00:05:22,456
Keep emergency money in a normal savings
account, and let your TFSA do the long,

96
00:05:22,476 --> 00:05:23,643
slow work.

97
00:05:23,663 --> 00:05:25,629
What if you find a better provider?

98
00:05:25,649 --> 00:05:27,069
You don't have to withdraw.

99
00:05:27,089 --> 00:05:28,456
You can transfer.

100
00:05:28,476 --> 00:05:31,816
Ask your new provider to
move your TFSA across.

101
00:05:31,836 --> 00:05:36,536
A proper transfer is not a new contribution,
so it doesn't touch your limits.

102
00:05:36,556 --> 00:05:40,296
The rules say it must be done within
ten business days of your request.

103
00:05:40,316 --> 00:05:45,816
What you must never do is withdraw the money
yourself and deposit it somewhere else.

104
00:05:45,836 --> 00:05:48,856
That counts as a brand new contribution.

105
00:05:49,645 --> 00:05:52,497
So what should you put inside your TFSA?

106
00:05:52,517 --> 00:05:54,297
There are two main options.

107
00:05:54,317 --> 00:05:58,457
A cash option, like a bank
savings or fixed deposit account.

108
00:05:58,477 --> 00:06:03,737
It's simple and safe, but the growth is
usually slow, and fixed terms can lock your

109
00:06:03,757 --> 00:06:05,977
money for up to five years.

110
00:06:05,997 --> 00:06:10,297
Or an investment option,
like a unit trust or an ETF.

111
00:06:10,317 --> 00:06:15,497
An ETF can follow a whole stock market
index, so one purchase spreads your money

112
00:06:15,517 --> 00:06:17,710
across many companies at once.

113
00:06:17,730 --> 00:06:23,017
The value goes up and down, sometimes a
lot, but over long periods it has more room

114
00:06:23,037 --> 00:06:24,084
to grow.

115
00:06:24,104 --> 00:06:28,697
That's why many people use their TFSA for
money they won't need for ten years or

116
00:06:28,797 --> 00:06:29,257
more.

117
00:06:29,277 --> 00:06:35,097
One thing you can't do: buy individual
company shares directly inside a TFSA.

118
00:06:35,117 --> 00:06:37,497
You invest through funds instead.

119
00:06:37,517 --> 00:06:39,710
The rules also protect you.

120
00:06:39,730 --> 00:06:44,737
Providers can't charge performance fees
on a TFSA, fees must be reasonable, and

121
00:06:44,757 --> 00:06:48,244
withdrawals must be paid out
within seven business days.

122
00:06:48,264 --> 00:06:53,097
But fees still differ between products,
so always compare before you choose.

123
00:06:53,117 --> 00:06:58,564
A common question: should you use
a TFSA or a retirement annuity?

124
00:06:58,584 --> 00:07:00,537
They work in opposite ways.

125
00:07:00,557 --> 00:07:05,257
A retirement annuity gives you a tax
deduction now, when you pay in, but you're

126
00:07:05,277 --> 00:07:09,950
taxed when you take it out, and most of
your money stays locked until retirement.

127
00:07:09,970 --> 00:07:15,457
A TFSA gives you no deduction now, but
everything comes out tax-free, and you can

128
00:07:15,477 --> 00:07:17,657
reach your money if you really need to.

129
00:07:17,677 --> 00:07:19,017
They're not rivals.

130
00:07:19,037 --> 00:07:23,937
Many people use both: the retirement
annuity for retirement, and the TFSA for

131
00:07:23,957 --> 00:07:26,297
long-term goals and extra freedom.

132
00:07:26,317 --> 00:07:29,284
You can also open a
TFSA for your children.

133
00:07:29,304 --> 00:07:33,417
A child's TFSA uses the
child's own limits, not yours.

134
00:07:33,437 --> 00:07:38,777
So you're using up part of their R500,000
lifetime limit, which can give them a huge

135
00:07:38,797 --> 00:07:39,897
head start.

136
00:07:39,917 --> 00:07:44,217
Just remember: when money is withdrawn,
it has to be paid into an account in the

137
00:07:44,237 --> 00:07:45,497
child's name.

138
00:07:45,517 --> 00:07:46,777
Ready to open one?

139
00:07:46,797 --> 00:07:47,817
Here's how.

140
00:07:47,837 --> 00:07:50,004
Step one: choose a provider.

141
00:07:50,024 --> 00:07:52,857
That can be your bank, or
an investment platform.

142
00:07:52,877 --> 00:07:58,137
Step two: have your ID ready, and possibly
a proof of address, for the normal FICA

143
00:07:58,237 --> 00:07:58,697
checks.

144
00:07:58,717 --> 00:08:04,217
Step three: choose what goes inside,
cash or investments, and check the fees.

145
00:08:04,237 --> 00:08:10,937
Step four: set up a debit order that stays
under the limit, like R3,833 a month.

146
00:08:10,957 --> 00:08:12,404
And watch the deadline.

147
00:08:12,424 --> 00:08:16,777
The tax year ends on the last day of
February, and some providers stop accepting

148
00:08:16,797 --> 00:08:18,857
deposits a few days before that.

149
00:08:18,877 --> 00:08:20,004
Let's recap.

150
00:08:20,024 --> 00:08:23,790
A TFSA is a wrapper that
protects your money from tax.

151
00:08:23,810 --> 00:08:28,697
You can put in R46,000 a
year, and R500,000 for life.

152
00:08:28,717 --> 00:08:31,417
Growth doesn't count towards the limits.

153
00:08:31,437 --> 00:08:36,057
Don't go over the limit, don't treat it
like an emergency fund, and if you switch

154
00:08:36,077 --> 00:08:39,417
providers, transfer
instead of withdrawing.

155
00:08:39,437 --> 00:08:42,737
Start small if you have
to, but start early.

156
00:08:42,757 --> 00:08:44,777
Your future self will thank you.

157
00:08:44,797 --> 00:08:47,777
If this helped, subscribe
to Money From Zero.

158
00:08:47,797 --> 00:08:50,777
Every week, we make one
more piece of money simple.

159
00:08:50,797 --> 00:08:54,737
This video is general
information, not financial advice.
